WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:08.196 --> 00:08.506 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

00:08.506 --> 00:09.286 align:middle
Good afternoon, everyone.

00:13.576 --> 00:17.336 align:middle
To begin, I'd like to say a few
words about Paul Volcker, who,

00:17.336 --> 00:19.076 align:middle
as you know, passed
away earlier this week.

00:20.136 --> 00:24.696 align:middle
Paul Volcker served as Federal
Reserve Chair from 1979 to 1987.

00:25.366 --> 00:27.286 align:middle
He accomplished many
things during his long

00:27.286 --> 00:29.396 align:middle
and distinguished career
at the Fed and elsewhere.

00:30.286 --> 00:32.376 align:middle
Of course, he's best known
for leading the fight

00:32.376 --> 00:34.916 align:middle
to tame the double-digit
inflation that he inherited

00:34.916 --> 00:37.986 align:middle
as Chair, thus laying the
foundation for the prosperity

00:37.986 --> 00:39.806 align:middle
and price stability
we enjoy today.

00:41.496 --> 00:44.096 align:middle
But what is perhaps most
admirable about him-more

00:44.096 --> 00:45.676 align:middle
than his many
accomplishments-was

00:45.676 --> 00:46.246 align:middle
his character.

00:47.156 --> 00:48.946 align:middle
He believed that there
is no higher calling

00:48.946 --> 00:49.826 align:middle
than public service,

00:50.386 --> 00:53.126 align:middle
and he dedicated the lion's
share of his life to it.

00:53.576 --> 00:55.566 align:middle
With courage, integrity,
and tenacity,

00:55.746 --> 00:57.326 align:middle
he always pursued the policies

00:57.326 --> 00:59.126 align:middle
that he believed would
ultimately benefit

00:59.206 --> 00:59.916 align:middle
all Americans.

01:00.946 --> 01:02.106 align:middle
My colleagues and I continue

01:02.106 --> 01:04.416 align:middle
to draw inspiration
from his example.

01:06.166 --> 01:08.306 align:middle
Turning to today's meeting,
my colleagues and I decided

01:08.306 --> 01:10.096 align:middle
to leave our policy
rate unchanged

01:10.266 --> 01:13.156 align:middle
after lowering it a total
of percentage point

01:13.156 --> 01:14.626 align:middle
at the previous three meetings.

01:15.386 --> 01:19.546 align:middle
As always, we base our decisions
on judgment of how best

01:19.546 --> 01:21.466 align:middle
to achieve the goals
Congress has given us:

01:21.726 --> 01:23.536 align:middle
maximum employment
and price stability.

01:25.276 --> 01:28.356 align:middle
Our economic outlook remains
a favorable one despite global

01:28.356 --> 01:29.906 align:middle
developments and ongoing risks.

01:30.846 --> 01:33.146 align:middle
With our decisions through
the course of the past year,

01:33.286 --> 01:35.566 align:middle
we believe that monetary
policy is well positioned

01:35.566 --> 01:36.796 align:middle
to serve the American people

01:37.216 --> 01:40.486 align:middle
by supporting continued economic
growth, a strong job market,

01:40.526 --> 01:43.306 align:middle
and inflation near our
symmetric 2 percent goal.

01:43.846 --> 01:48.666 align:middle
The economic expansion
is in its 11th year,

01:48.806 --> 01:49.766 align:middle
the longest on record.

01:50.186 --> 01:51.836 align:middle
Household spending
has been strong,

01:51.836 --> 01:54.586 align:middle
supported by a healthy job
market, rising incomes,

01:54.626 --> 01:56.156 align:middle
and solid consumer confidence.

01:57.396 --> 02:00.256 align:middle
In contrast, business investment
and exports remain weak,

02:00.846 --> 02:03.756 align:middle
and manufacturing output has
declined over the past year.

02:04.276 --> 02:08.656 align:middle
As has been the case for some
time, sluggish growth abroad

02:08.656 --> 02:11.076 align:middle
and trade developments have
been weighing on those sectors.

02:12.026 --> 02:14.756 align:middle
Even so, the overall economy
has been growing moderately.

02:15.066 --> 02:18.756 align:middle
And with a strong household
sector and supportive monetary

02:18.756 --> 02:19.826 align:middle
and financial conditions,

02:19.826 --> 02:21.836 align:middle
we expect moderate
growth to continue.

02:23.386 --> 02:26.906 align:middle
As seen from FOMC participants'
most recent projections,

02:27.476 --> 02:31.586 align:middle
the median expectation for
real GDP growth slows slightly

02:31.586 --> 02:34.096 align:middle
over the next few years
but remains near 2 percent.

02:35.906 --> 02:38.626 align:middle
The unemployment rate has
been near half-century lows

02:38.626 --> 02:40.826 align:middle
for well more than
a year, and the pace

02:40.866 --> 02:42.546 align:middle
of job gains remains solid.

02:43.976 --> 02:46.216 align:middle
Participation in the
labor force by people

02:46.216 --> 02:47.526 align:middle
in their prime working years,

02:47.526 --> 02:50.556 align:middle
ages 25 through 54,
has been increasing.

02:51.316 --> 02:53.176 align:middle
And wages have been
rising, particularly

02:53.176 --> 02:54.716 align:middle
for lower-paying jobs.

02:55.716 --> 02:57.056 align:middle
People who live and work in low-

02:57.056 --> 02:59.356 align:middle
and middle-income
communities tell us that many

02:59.356 --> 03:01.636 align:middle
who have struggled to
find work are now finding

03:01.636 --> 03:02.516 align:middle
new opportunities.

03:03.766 --> 03:06.526 align:middle
Employment gains have been
broad based across all racial

03:06.526 --> 03:08.976 align:middle
and ethnic groups and
all levels of education.

03:10.106 --> 03:12.396 align:middle
These developments underscore
for us the importance

03:12.396 --> 03:13.666 align:middle
of sustaining the expansion

03:14.156 --> 03:16.356 align:middle
so that the strong job
market reaches more

03:16.356 --> 03:17.276 align:middle
of those left behind.

03:17.776 --> 03:20.646 align:middle
We expect the job
market to remain strong.

03:21.086 --> 03:22.876 align:middle
The median of participants'
projections

03:22.876 --> 03:25.896 align:middle
for the unemployment rate
remains below 4 percent

03:25.956 --> 03:27.116 align:middle
over the next several years.

03:29.056 --> 03:31.786 align:middle
Inflation continues to
run below our symmetric 2

03:31.786 --> 03:32.546 align:middle
percent objective.

03:33.066 --> 03:35.136 align:middle
Over the 12 months
through October,

03:35.496 --> 03:38.066 align:middle
total PCE inflation
was 1.3 percent,

03:38.566 --> 03:41.526 align:middle
and core inflation-which
excludes volatile food

03:41.526 --> 03:43.536 align:middle
and energy prices and
is a better indicator

03:43.536 --> 03:46.546 align:middle
of future inflation-was
1.6 percent.

03:47.086 --> 03:50.856 align:middle
While low and stable inflation
is certainly a good thing,

03:50.906 --> 03:54.256 align:middle
inflation that runs persistently
below our objective can lead

03:54.256 --> 03:57.176 align:middle
to an unhealthy dynamic in
which longer-term inflation

03:57.176 --> 04:00.936 align:middle
expectations drift down, pulling
actual inflation even lower.

04:01.776 --> 04:04.286 align:middle
In turn, interest rates would
be lower as well and closer

04:04.286 --> 04:05.616 align:middle
to their effective lower bound.

04:06.226 --> 04:08.796 align:middle
As a result, the scope for
interest rate reductions

04:08.796 --> 04:09.786 align:middle
to support the economy

04:09.786 --> 04:11.706 align:middle
in a future downturn
would be diminished,

04:12.506 --> 04:14.486 align:middle
resulting in worse
economic outcomes

04:14.946 --> 04:16.706 align:middle
for American families
and businesses.

04:17.216 --> 04:21.076 align:middle
Against the backdrop
of a strong economy

04:21.206 --> 04:22.986 align:middle
and supportive monetary policy,

04:23.146 --> 04:25.286 align:middle
we expect inflation
will rise to 2 percent.

04:26.166 --> 04:28.196 align:middle
The median of participants'
projection rises

04:28.196 --> 04:31.426 align:middle
to 1.9 percent next year
and 2 percent in 2021.

04:32.246 --> 04:33.186 align:middle
We're strongly committed

04:33.186 --> 04:35.586 align:middle
to achieving our symmetric
2 percent inflation goal.

04:36.066 --> 04:39.976 align:middle
Over the course of the past
year, our views about the path

04:39.976 --> 04:42.296 align:middle
of interest rates that would
best achieve our employment

04:42.296 --> 04:44.586 align:middle
and inflation objectives
changed significantly,

04:45.196 --> 04:47.346 align:middle
as the economy faced
some important challenges

04:47.376 --> 04:49.626 align:middle
from weaker global growth
and trade developments.

04:50.006 --> 04:52.566 align:middle
As the year progressed,
we adjusted the stance

04:52.566 --> 04:54.636 align:middle
of monetary policy to
cushion the economy

04:54.996 --> 04:57.596 align:middle
from these developments and
to provide some insurance

04:57.596 --> 04:59.076 align:middle
against the associated risks.

04:59.076 --> 05:03.396 align:middle
In addition, inflation pressures
were unexpectedly muted,

05:03.716 --> 05:04.696 align:middle
strengthening the case

05:04.696 --> 05:06.546 align:middle
for a more supportive
stance of policy.

05:08.476 --> 05:10.726 align:middle
Rather than modestly
increasing the target rate

05:10.726 --> 05:12.216 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate this year,

05:12.216 --> 05:15.166 align:middle
as seemed appropriate a
year ago, we reduced it

05:15.166 --> 05:16.666 align:middle
by percentage point.

05:17.306 --> 05:19.746 align:middle
This shift has helped
support the economy

05:19.796 --> 05:21.526 align:middle
and has kept the
outlook on track.

05:22.556 --> 05:25.136 align:middle
The medians of participants'
projections for economic growth,

05:25.476 --> 05:28.046 align:middle
the unemployment rate, and
inflation are little changed

05:28.146 --> 05:29.666 align:middle
from a year ago, aside

05:29.666 --> 05:32.246 align:middle
from a lower inflation
projection for 2019.

05:32.986 --> 05:34.426 align:middle
Of course, that is the function

05:34.426 --> 05:37.026 align:middle
of monetary policy-to
adjust interest rates

05:37.026 --> 05:40.186 align:middle
to promote employment and
price stability in response

05:40.186 --> 05:41.856 align:middle
to forces acting on the economy.

05:42.426 --> 05:45.196 align:middle
We believe that the
current stance

05:45.196 --> 05:47.396 align:middle
of monetary policy will
support sustained growth,

05:47.756 --> 05:48.786 align:middle
a strong labor market,

05:48.826 --> 05:51.906 align:middle
and inflation near our
symmetric 2 percent objective.

05:52.336 --> 05:54.336 align:middle
As long as incoming information

05:54.336 --> 05:56.586 align:middle
about the economy
remains broadly consistent

05:56.586 --> 05:58.796 align:middle
with this outlook,
the current stance

05:58.796 --> 06:01.466 align:middle
of monetary policy likely
will remain appropriate.

06:02.526 --> 06:05.076 align:middle
Looking ahead, we will
be monitoring the effects

06:05.076 --> 06:06.466 align:middle
of our recent policy actions,

06:06.466 --> 06:09.266 align:middle
along with other information
bearing on the outlook,

06:09.706 --> 06:12.126 align:middle
as we assess the appropriate
path of the target range

06:12.126 --> 06:13.116 align:middle
for the federal funds rate.

06:13.796 --> 06:15.316 align:middle
Of course, if developments
emerge

06:15.316 --> 06:17.686 align:middle
that cause a material
reassessment of our outlook,

06:18.176 --> 06:19.386 align:middle
we would respond accordingly.

06:20.106 --> 06:21.916 align:middle
Policy is not on
a preset course.

06:23.676 --> 06:24.836 align:middle
Finally, I wanted to note

06:24.836 --> 06:26.366 align:middle
that we've been purchasing
Treasury bills

06:26.366 --> 06:28.306 align:middle
and conducting repurchase
operations,

06:28.306 --> 06:30.356 align:middle
consistent with the plan
we announced in December.

06:31.196 --> 06:32.786 align:middle
These technical operations
are aimed

06:32.786 --> 06:34.756 align:middle
at maintaining an
ample level of reserves

06:34.756 --> 06:36.296 align:middle
and addressing money
market pressures

06:36.336 --> 06:38.386 align:middle
that could adversely
affect the implementation

06:38.386 --> 06:39.376 align:middle
of monetary policy.

06:39.836 --> 06:44.176 align:middle
Our operations have gone well so
far; pressures in money markets

06:44.236 --> 06:46.316 align:middle
over recent weeks
have been subdued.

06:47.196 --> 06:50.126 align:middle
To address possible pressures in
money markets over the year-end,

06:50.326 --> 06:53.536 align:middle
we've been conducting term repo
operations spanning year-end.

06:54.236 --> 06:56.786 align:middle
We stand ready to adjust the
details of our operations

06:56.786 --> 06:58.726 align:middle
as appropriate to keep
the federal funds rate

06:59.336 --> 07:00.136 align:middle
in the target range.

07:00.926 --> 07:02.706 align:middle
Thank you, and I'll be happy
to take your questions.

07:02.916 --> 07:03.836 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

07:04.256 --> 07:07.056 align:middle
[Inaudible remark] Sorry-Howard
Schneider with Reuters.

07:07.556 --> 07:09.246 align:middle
Sorry about that.

07:09.246 --> 07:14.886 align:middle
So I was struck by the, sort
of, disconnect that exists here

07:14.886 --> 07:19.066 align:middle
between the behavior of
unemployment and inflation.

07:19.066 --> 07:21.216 align:middle
You seem to have unemployment
penciled in here now

07:21.216 --> 07:24.286 align:middle
for three years
running-more-underneath the

07:24.286 --> 07:29.126 align:middle
longer-run level, yet inflation
never really accelerates.

07:29.126 --> 07:30.636 align:middle
So what are we to make of that?

07:30.746 --> 07:31.156 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

07:31.476 --> 07:36.126 align:middle
Well, that's-what's
happening there is the fact

07:36.556 --> 07:40.456 align:middle
that the relationship
between resource utilization,

07:40.456 --> 07:46.516 align:middle
or unemployment, and inflation
has just gotten weaker

07:46.516 --> 07:47.386 align:middle
and weaker over the years.

07:47.386 --> 07:49.366 align:middle
If you go back 50 years,
you would have seen that,

07:49.776 --> 07:51.946 align:middle
when there was-when
labor markets were tight

07:51.996 --> 07:54.446 align:middle
and unemployment was low,
inflation moved right up.

07:54.856 --> 07:57.236 align:middle
And then as the Fed got
control of inflation,

07:57.236 --> 08:00.396 align:middle
the connection got weaker and
weaker and weaker, to the point

08:00.396 --> 08:01.556 align:middle
where there's still
a connection,

08:02.066 --> 08:03.666 align:middle
but it's-it's a very faint one.

08:03.666 --> 08:05.896 align:middle
And I-what that suggests
is that you would need

08:05.896 --> 08:08.486 align:middle
to keep policy somewhat
accommodative.

08:08.486 --> 08:10.606 align:middle
And we believe that policy
is somewhat accommodative,

08:11.246 --> 08:13.996 align:middle
and we think that that's the
appropriate place for policy

08:13.996 --> 08:16.196 align:middle
to be in order to
drive up inflation.

08:16.856 --> 08:18.396 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

08:18.936 --> 08:21.676 align:middle
If I could follow up on that-I
mean, it's faint to nonexistent,

08:21.706 --> 08:23.986 align:middle
though, it seems to me, so
this suggests that, really,

08:23.986 --> 08:27.226 align:middle
your policy lever as it exists
now isn't really influencing

08:27.226 --> 08:28.156 align:middle
prices at all.

08:28.616 --> 08:30.986 align:middle
So, in that case, what do
you do to reach your target?

08:30.986 --> 08:31.426 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:31.426 --> 08:32.426 align:middle
You know, I don't
think that's right.

08:32.426 --> 08:37.106 align:middle
I mean, we-the-again, the
relationship between slack

08:37.106 --> 08:40.256 align:middle
in the economy and inflation
is weak, has been weak.

08:40.606 --> 08:42.526 align:middle
The coefficient is
something like 0.1.

08:43.116 --> 08:46.036 align:middle
So it's much, much lower than
it used to be, but I don't know

08:46.036 --> 08:48.386 align:middle
that it's gone down any
more in the last decade.

08:48.386 --> 08:49.516 align:middle
It just is quite faint.

08:49.516 --> 08:50.296 align:middle
But it's still there.

08:50.296 --> 08:51.226 align:middle
I mean, you can see it.

08:51.226 --> 08:55.026 align:middle
If you look-look at where wages
were three or four years ago,

08:55.026 --> 08:56.396 align:middle
they were running
around 2 percent.

08:56.816 --> 09:01.006 align:middle
And now the whole group
of different wage measures

09:01.006 --> 09:04.186 align:middle
that we monitor has moved
up to 3 and 3½ percent,

09:04.186 --> 09:05.436 align:middle
and that suggests tightening.

09:05.956 --> 09:10.406 align:middle
The same thing is true, but
much less true, of inflation,

09:10.406 --> 09:12.576 align:middle
the relationship
between-in a way,

09:12.576 --> 09:15.876 align:middle
the wage Phillips curve
has a higher coefficient

09:15.876 --> 09:17.296 align:middle
than the price Phillips
curve does.

09:17.996 --> 09:21.296 align:middle
But we do still see
some relationship,

09:21.296 --> 09:22.656 align:middle
and that's what you're
seeing in those numbers.

09:23.826 --> 09:24.586 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

09:26.746 --> 09:28.556 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, Steve
Liesman CNBC.

09:28.826 --> 09:31.236 align:middle
You've used the analogy to 1998

09:31.236 --> 09:33.206 align:middle
to describe the rate cuts
we just went through,

09:33.756 --> 09:36.636 align:middle
and I'm wondering if we can
sort of continue the analogy.

09:36.696 --> 09:39.126 align:middle
Within seven months of
those '98 rate cuts,

09:39.636 --> 09:41.636 align:middle
the Fed took them
back and then some.

09:42.416 --> 09:45.226 align:middle
So, were these those
kind of rate cuts,

09:45.266 --> 09:48.746 align:middle
the kind that you need to take
back, or are we at a place now

09:48.746 --> 09:50.996 align:middle
where we're at a
neutral rate-"take back,"

09:50.996 --> 09:54.296 align:middle
I mean if those risks that
you say we're cushioning

09:54.296 --> 09:55.476 align:middle
from don't materialize

09:55.476 --> 09:59.926 align:middle
in the way you think-or is this
now a new sort of steady state

10:00.486 --> 10:03.216 align:middle
for the economy at this
rate, this is the right rate

10:03.476 --> 10:05.996 align:middle
for the economy that you see
going forward and don't need

10:05.996 --> 10:07.096 align:middle
to take those rate cuts back?

10:07.096 --> 10:07.366 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:08.146 --> 10:11.886 align:middle
So there's similarities,
conceptual similarities,

10:11.886 --> 10:14.106 align:middle
I think, between the
two instances during

10:14.106 --> 10:16.546 align:middle
that long expansion, I
guess in '95 and '98,

10:16.546 --> 10:20.446 align:middle
when the Fed cut three times
only to resume raising rates.

10:20.446 --> 10:25.626 align:middle
And the notion just is that it's
a-the economy needed slightly

10:25.626 --> 10:27.746 align:middle
more accommodative policy,
but it wasn't the end

10:27.746 --> 10:28.816 align:middle
of the expansion, right?

10:29.286 --> 10:31.986 align:middle
And that's the same situation
we believe we're in here.

10:31.986 --> 10:35.416 align:middle
So this is that we did, in fact,
turn out to do three rate cuts.

10:35.416 --> 10:37.286 align:middle
That wasn't in the
plan, you know,

10:37.286 --> 10:38.776 align:middle
in any kind of specific
way at the beginning.

10:39.226 --> 10:40.106 align:middle
So that's the same.

10:40.346 --> 10:44.916 align:middle
What's different is, you have
a very different-you have very

10:44.916 --> 10:47.576 align:middle
different structural
characteristics in the economy,

10:47.576 --> 10:48.916 align:middle
particularly around inflation.

10:49.586 --> 10:54.196 align:middle
So now, as you can see,
inflation is barely moving up,

10:54.346 --> 10:57.726 align:middle
notwithstanding that
unemployment is at 50-year lows

10:58.066 --> 10:59.306 align:middle
and expected to remain there.

10:59.786 --> 11:03.446 align:middle
So the need for rate
increases is less.

11:03.836 --> 11:06.576 align:middle
And, by the way, this is-it's
a good thing that, you know,

11:06.576 --> 11:08.406 align:middle
we think we can-I
think we've learned

11:08.866 --> 11:12.046 align:middle
that unemployment can
remain at quite low levels

11:12.596 --> 11:14.056 align:middle
for an extended period of time

11:14.056 --> 11:17.716 align:middle
without unwanted upward
pressure on inflation.

11:17.856 --> 11:20.646 align:middle
In fact, we need some
upward pressure on inflation

11:20.646 --> 11:21.876 align:middle
to get back to 2 percent.

11:21.876 --> 11:25.086 align:middle
It's quite different
in '95 and '98

11:25.086 --> 11:27.066 align:middle
when there were those
two adjustments.

11:27.396 --> 11:29.206 align:middle
So, I would say,
similarities and differences.

11:31.046 --> 11:31.726 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

11:32.036 --> 11:37.066 align:middle
The BIS concluded in-I did
the same thing as Howard.

11:37.096 --> 11:39.226 align:middle
Michael McKee, Bloomberg
radio and television.

11:39.926 --> 11:41.676 align:middle
The BIS concluded in September

11:41.676 --> 11:45.156 align:middle
that the repo spike was
not a one-off confluence

11:45.156 --> 11:49.146 align:middle
of random events but reflected
structural and regulatory issues

11:49.146 --> 11:51.046 align:middle
that could lead to a recurrence.

11:51.046 --> 11:53.726 align:middle
I'd like to ask you if you
agree with the BIS findings.

11:53.726 --> 11:56.236 align:middle
And, given that we're
approaching year-end

11:56.236 --> 12:00.516 align:middle
for the markets, will you be
taking any extra steps to ensure

12:00.516 --> 12:03.856 align:middle
that funding is available in
the repo and FX swaps markets?

12:04.096 --> 12:05.266 align:middle
There was a report yesterday

12:05.526 --> 12:08.106 align:middle
from Credit Suisse suggesting
there's a good chance

12:08.446 --> 12:12.526 align:middle
that we will see disruptions,
and one of the reasons they put

12:12.526 --> 12:18.176 align:middle
for it is that the Fed is at
this point buying only T-bills,

12:18.176 --> 12:20.286 align:middle
and the market wants
to sell coupons.

12:20.476 --> 12:22.906 align:middle
Do you have any plans
to sell coupons?

12:23.106 --> 12:23.536 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:23.536 --> 12:26.116 align:middle
So I'm going to take a little
step back, and I will-I will get

12:26.116 --> 12:28.846 align:middle
to your specific questions on
the year-end and on T-bills.

12:29.166 --> 12:32.186 align:middle
So I guess I want to
start by stressing

12:32.186 --> 12:34.356 align:middle
that these are very
important operational matters

12:34.756 --> 12:37.696 align:middle
but that are not likely to have
any macroeconomic implications.

12:38.226 --> 12:42.716 align:middle
We'd decided back in January to
remain an ample-reserves regime,

12:42.716 --> 12:45.246 align:middle
and that means we will be
setting the federal funds

12:45.246 --> 12:46.836 align:middle
rate-the range for
the federal funds rate

12:46.836 --> 12:48.176 align:middle
through our administered
rates and not

12:48.176 --> 12:50.196 align:middle
through active management
of the level of reserves.

12:50.406 --> 12:53.446 align:middle
We're committed to robustly
implementing that framework,

12:53.446 --> 12:55.446 align:middle
as you can see by our actions.

12:56.056 --> 12:59.266 align:middle
And the purpose of all this,
let's remember, is to assure

12:59.266 --> 13:01.566 align:middle
that our monetary policy
decisions will be transmitted

13:01.566 --> 13:02.686 align:middle
to the federal funds rate,

13:02.976 --> 13:04.656 align:middle
which in turn affects
other short-term rates.

13:05.056 --> 13:08.216 align:middle
We have the tools to accomplish
that, and we will use them.

13:08.406 --> 13:12.006 align:middle
The purpose of all this is not
to eliminate all volatility,

13:12.006 --> 13:13.186 align:middle
particularly in the repo market.

13:13.796 --> 13:18.346 align:middle
So, taking you back,
this-as you know,

13:18.346 --> 13:23.236 align:middle
we had very gradually allowed
the balance sheet to shrink,

13:23.776 --> 13:26.656 align:middle
we slowed that gradual
pace by half in March,

13:26.656 --> 13:27.976 align:middle
and then we ended it in July.

13:28.616 --> 13:30.976 align:middle
Meanwhile, we had
surveyed all of the banks,

13:30.976 --> 13:32.976 align:middle
and particularly the large banks
who hold a lot of the reserves,

13:32.976 --> 13:35.326 align:middle
and said, "What's your lowest
comfortable level of reserves?"

13:35.726 --> 13:37.886 align:middle
We got those numbers, we added
them up, we added a buffer,

13:38.436 --> 13:40.726 align:middle
and it came out sort of at
a level that was well below

13:40.726 --> 13:41.796 align:middle
where we were in September.

13:42.116 --> 13:44.376 align:middle
And yet we saw, actually,
in September

13:44.376 --> 13:46.196 align:middle
that reserves-the markets acted

13:46.196 --> 13:47.726 align:middle
as though reserves
had become scarce.

13:48.216 --> 13:50.146 align:middle
So what had happened
was that liquidity,

13:50.146 --> 13:52.926 align:middle
which actually existed, didn't
flow into the repo market,

13:53.186 --> 13:54.766 align:middle
and that had effects on
the federal funds rate.

13:55.176 --> 13:57.086 align:middle
So the question is,
why did that happen?

13:57.286 --> 14:00.746 align:middle
And we've been very carefully
looking at the reasons why

14:00.746 --> 14:01.496 align:middle
that might have happened.

14:01.496 --> 14:05.176 align:middle
There are-there are payments
issues, there have been a number

14:05.176 --> 14:07.766 align:middle
of supervisory and regulatory
issues raised-we're looking

14:07.766 --> 14:08.756 align:middle
carefully at those.

14:09.126 --> 14:12.466 align:middle
We're open to ideas for
modifying supervisory

14:12.466 --> 14:16.936 align:middle
and regulatory practice in ways
that don't undermine safety

14:16.936 --> 14:18.226 align:middle
and soundness, and a number

14:18.226 --> 14:20.066 align:middle
of ideas are under-under
examination there.

14:20.646 --> 14:24.676 align:middle
To go through it sort of by-in
time, we started off, really,

14:24.676 --> 14:27.566 align:middle
on September 17 with
overnight operations.

14:28.046 --> 14:31.786 align:middle
By October 11 we had created
and put into effect a plan.

14:31.786 --> 14:33.176 align:middle
That plan is in effect.

14:34.036 --> 14:35.316 align:middle
It's working.

14:35.646 --> 14:37.476 align:middle
I think, for the last
couple of months,

14:37.826 --> 14:39.486 align:middle
repo markets have
been functioning well,

14:39.796 --> 14:43.666 align:middle
short-term rates are stable,
markets are functioning.

14:43.666 --> 14:45.796 align:middle
So you asked about year-end.

14:46.066 --> 14:49.396 align:middle
Temporary upward pressures

14:49.396 --> 14:52.816 align:middle
on short-term money market rates
are not unusual around year-end.

14:53.436 --> 14:55.796 align:middle
And our-both our repo operations

14:55.796 --> 14:59.046 align:middle
and Treasury bill purchases are
intended to mitigate the risks

14:59.046 --> 15:01.086 align:middle
that such pressures
pose to our control

15:01.086 --> 15:03.366 align:middle
of the federal funds rate.

15:03.716 --> 15:05.976 align:middle
We think that the
pressures appear manageable,

15:06.426 --> 15:09.136 align:middle
and we stand ready to adjust
the details of our operations

15:09.136 --> 15:12.166 align:middle
as necessary to keep the federal
funds rate in the target range.

15:12.976 --> 15:16.066 align:middle
Our strategy has
been-essentially,

15:16.066 --> 15:19.566 align:middle
the key to our strategy
is to supply reserves

15:19.566 --> 15:22.656 align:middle
in the near term through-through
both overnight and term repo,

15:23.266 --> 15:26.336 align:middle
and, at the same time, we're
raising the underlying level

15:26.336 --> 15:27.976 align:middle
of reserves through
bill purchases.

15:28.116 --> 15:28.996 align:middle
I'll take that now.

15:29.346 --> 15:30.856 align:middle
We've said bills-bill purchases.

15:30.856 --> 15:33.126 align:middle
We've also said that we were
willing to adapt our strategy.

15:33.716 --> 15:36.156 align:middle
We're not at this place, but
if it does become appropriate

15:36.156 --> 15:40.506 align:middle
for us to purchase other
short-term coupon securities,

15:41.016 --> 15:43.526 align:middle
then we would be prepared to
do that if-if the need arises.

15:44.106 --> 15:45.846 align:middle
So-but we don't-we
don't-we're not in that place.

15:45.846 --> 15:47.026 align:middle
It looks-it very much looks

15:47.026 --> 15:50.026 align:middle
like the bill-so those bill
purchases are going well,

15:50.546 --> 15:52.016 align:middle
just according to expectations.

15:52.566 --> 15:55.466 align:middle
I mean, the other thing I'll
say is that we're in, you know,

15:55.556 --> 15:58.636 align:middle
very regular contact with market
participants all the time.

15:58.816 --> 16:01.376 align:middle
We'll be providing-we'll
be continuing that,

16:01.506 --> 16:03.856 align:middle
and we're prepared to
adjust our tactics.

16:03.856 --> 16:06.666 align:middle
We're-we're focused on-on
year-end as well and prepared

16:06.786 --> 16:09.146 align:middle
to adjust our operations
as appropriate.

16:09.186 --> 16:09.646 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

16:09.646 --> 16:11.326 align:middle
The follow-up to that
would be, where are we

16:11.326 --> 16:13.716 align:middle
with the possibility of
a standing repo facility?

16:13.716 --> 16:14.016 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:14.326 --> 16:17.226 align:middle
So, on the standing repo
facility, as you know,

16:17.226 --> 16:18.946 align:middle
we've actually had
a couple of meetings

16:18.946 --> 16:20.856 align:middle
where we've discussed that.

16:21.346 --> 16:24.936 align:middle
I think the standing repo
facility is something that'll

16:24.936 --> 16:28.266 align:middle
take some time to evaluate
and create the parameters

16:28.266 --> 16:29.366 align:middle
of and put into place.

16:29.996 --> 16:32.396 align:middle
At the moment what
we're focused on is,

16:32.566 --> 16:34.036 align:middle
you know-we're now
focused on year-end.

16:35.026 --> 16:37.776 align:middle
I should also mention that after
the year-end, the sense of it is

16:37.776 --> 16:42.466 align:middle
that as the underlying level
of reserves moves up because

16:42.466 --> 16:46.136 align:middle
of bill purchases, as that
happens, there will come a time

16:46.136 --> 16:48.876 align:middle
when it will be appropriate
for overnight

16:48.876 --> 16:51.056 align:middle
and term repo to
gradually decline.

16:51.306 --> 16:53.906 align:middle
We don't know-we can't
know today what the timing

16:53.906 --> 16:57.016 align:middle
of that will be, but that's the
way-that's the way we see it

16:57.016 --> 17:00.456 align:middle
going over time.

17:00.916 --> 17:02.406 align:middle
HEATHER LONG.

17:04.226 --> 17:06.386 align:middle
Heather Long from
the Washington Post.

17:06.606 --> 17:09.896 align:middle
A number of your colleagues
have said explicitly

17:09.896 --> 17:13.566 align:middle
that they do not think the FOMC
should raise interest rates

17:13.566 --> 17:16.536 align:middle
until core inflation
is back at 2 percent.

17:16.576 --> 17:17.836 align:middle
They think there
should be a rule.

17:18.086 --> 17:20.096 align:middle
Where do you personally
stand on that?

17:20.636 --> 17:21.206 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:21.336 --> 17:25.526 align:middle
Well, as I mentioned, actually,
at the last press conference,

17:26.976 --> 17:29.896 align:middle
we think our policy
rate is appropriate

17:29.896 --> 17:31.676 align:middle
and will remain appropriate
as long

17:31.676 --> 17:34.496 align:middle
as incoming data are
broadly in keeping

17:34.496 --> 17:37.136 align:middle
with our-with our outlook.

17:37.846 --> 17:42.576 align:middle
And in order to move
rates up, I would want

17:42.576 --> 17:44.136 align:middle
to see inflation
that's persistent

17:45.076 --> 17:47.856 align:middle
and that's significant-a
significant move-up

17:47.856 --> 17:51.336 align:middle
in inflation that's also
persistent before raising rates

17:51.336 --> 17:53.786 align:middle
to address inflation concerns.

17:53.966 --> 17:54.646 align:middle
That's my view.

17:55.346 --> 17:55.576 align:middle
HEATHER LONG.

17:55.576 --> 17:58.446 align:middle
So is this de facto policy
of the Committee then?

17:58.446 --> 18:00.576 align:middle
I guess I'm wondering
why it's not sort

18:00.576 --> 18:02.236 align:middle
of codified in a statement.

18:02.846 --> 18:03.586 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:05.626 --> 18:09.076 align:middle
We haven't-we haven't tried
to turn it into some sort of,

18:09.546 --> 18:12.066 align:middle
you know, official
forward guidance.

18:12.146 --> 18:14.276 align:middle
It happens to be my view that
that's what it would take

18:14.856 --> 18:18.226 align:middle
to want to move interest
rates up in order

18:18.226 --> 18:19.006 align:middle
to deal with inflation.

18:19.486 --> 18:20.816 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

18:22.246 --> 18:24.356 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

18:24.356 --> 18:25.746 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek with
the New York Times.

18:25.746 --> 18:28.916 align:middle
Just following up on Heather's
question-so if you look

18:28.916 --> 18:31.386 align:middle
at the SEP today, it looks

18:31.386 --> 18:34.296 align:middle
like inflation is never
overshooting 2 percent

18:34.296 --> 18:38.106 align:middle
and is only getting up to 2
percent by the end of 2021,

18:38.106 --> 18:40.406 align:middle
yet rates are increasing
in 2021.

18:40.406 --> 18:43.276 align:middle
So, I guess, how do
we square that circle,

18:43.646 --> 18:46.276 align:middle
with interest rates increasing
before inflation ever really

18:46.276 --> 18:48.486 align:middle
moves up in some
meaningful overshooting way?

18:48.996 --> 18:49.376 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:49.516 --> 18:52.636 align:middle
I think what you're seeing
is, you've got a full year

18:53.236 --> 18:56.886 align:middle
of the median being flat, which
is, we think, accommodative,

18:56.886 --> 18:58.966 align:middle
modestly accommodative,
and inflation not moving

18:58.966 --> 19:00.476 align:middle
up very much, as
we've discussed.

19:00.816 --> 19:02.486 align:middle
And that underscores,
I think, the challenge

19:02.896 --> 19:04.306 align:middle
of getting inflation to move up.

19:04.856 --> 19:07.626 align:middle
The Committee has wanted
inflation to be at 2 percent,

19:07.976 --> 19:12.626 align:middle
squarely at 2 percent, for-ever
since I arrived in 2012.

19:12.816 --> 19:14.666 align:middle
And it hasn't happened,
and it's just-it's

19:14.666 --> 19:16.236 align:middle
because there are
disinflationary forces

19:16.236 --> 19:18.426 align:middle
around the world, and they've
been stronger than, I think,

19:18.546 --> 19:21.636 align:middle
people understood them to be.

19:21.636 --> 19:23.896 align:middle
In terms of those
out-year rate increases,

19:23.896 --> 19:26.776 align:middle
what's you're-you're looking at
rate increases more than a year

19:26.776 --> 19:28.396 align:middle
into the future, and, you know,

19:28.396 --> 19:29.726 align:middle
people will have
their own explanations

19:29.726 --> 19:32.066 align:middle
for why they do that, but
we-really, none of us have much

19:32.066 --> 19:35.046 align:middle
of a sense of what the
economy will be like in 2021.

19:35.046 --> 19:36.856 align:middle
So I think what may
be behind some

19:36.856 --> 19:40.356 align:middle
of that is just the
thought that, over time,

19:40.586 --> 19:42.136 align:middle
it would be appropriate-if
you believe

19:42.136 --> 19:44.656 align:middle
that the neutral rate is 2½
percent, it would be appropriate

19:44.656 --> 19:46.976 align:middle
for your rates to move
up in that direction.

19:47.226 --> 19:50.216 align:middle
I will also say to you that a
number of people wrote down,

19:50.216 --> 19:52.956 align:middle
and you can't see this
at this level of detail,

19:52.956 --> 19:57.166 align:middle
but-today-but a number of
people did write down overshoots

19:57.166 --> 19:57.826 align:middle
of inflation

19:58.456 --> 20:00.266 align:middle
as appropriate-under
appropriate policy.

20:00.666 --> 20:03.086 align:middle
BRENDAN GREELEY.

20:03.086 --> 20:08.586 align:middle
Brendan Greeley from
the Financial Times.

20:09.036 --> 20:13.736 align:middle
Given that inflation-whichever
measure you choose-has either

20:13.736 --> 20:18.026 align:middle
sort of remained where it is or
not increased over the past year

20:18.556 --> 20:20.876 align:middle
as you've been dropping
the policy rate,

20:23.076 --> 20:25.156 align:middle
what is it that gives
you confidence

20:25.216 --> 20:29.146 align:middle
that the Fed has the tools to
get inflation back up to target

20:29.146 --> 20:31.226 align:middle
or even overshoot as
you just indicated

20:31.276 --> 20:32.686 align:middle
that some people are
considering doing?

20:33.726 --> 20:34.216 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:34.216 --> 20:37.716 align:middle
Well, it's just that there is
still, empirically, by many,

20:37.716 --> 20:40.356 align:middle
many different-by the work
of many different analysts,

20:40.356 --> 20:44.026 align:middle
there is a relationship
between resource utilization,

20:44.026 --> 20:45.716 align:middle
by which I mean unemployment,
and inflation.

20:45.766 --> 20:47.466 align:middle
It's just relatively weak.

20:47.826 --> 20:49.346 align:middle
And, by the way,
that's not a bad thing.

20:49.346 --> 20:53.776 align:middle
That means that we can run
at low levels of unemployment

20:53.776 --> 20:57.206 align:middle
and have a historically good-in
some dimensions-labor market

20:57.476 --> 20:58.846 align:middle
without having to
worry about inflation.

20:58.886 --> 21:02.426 align:middle
It also means, though, that it's
not easy to move inflation up.

21:02.426 --> 21:03.806 align:middle
Now, you say, why
is there confidence?

21:04.276 --> 21:05.836 align:middle
I mean, I would say
there's more humility

21:05.836 --> 21:07.756 align:middle
than there is confidence
in this at this point.

21:07.796 --> 21:12.036 align:middle
It's been very challenging to
get inflation to be at target.

21:12.036 --> 21:14.146 align:middle
If you look around the
world, the United States,

21:14.216 --> 21:17.276 align:middle
of all major economies,
has been closest to it

21:17.346 --> 21:19.156 align:middle
but still hasn't quite
been able to achieve it.

21:19.656 --> 21:21.856 align:middle
And I would also point
out that this year,

21:21.926 --> 21:23.586 align:middle
which has been a good
year for the economy,

21:23.946 --> 21:27.396 align:middle
inflation-core inflation is
actually running at 1.6 percent,

21:27.486 --> 21:30.276 align:middle
whereas it ran close to 2
percent most of last year.

21:30.926 --> 21:32.716 align:middle
So it's-it's a real challenge,

21:32.716 --> 21:36.056 align:middle
and-but I think we're using
our tools as best we can

21:36.296 --> 21:37.196 align:middle
to meet that challenge.

21:38.256 --> 21:39.036 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

21:40.686 --> 21:43.146 align:middle
Nick Timiraos, the
Wall Street Journal.

21:44.126 --> 21:46.646 align:middle
So, Chair Powell, I want to
ask about the framework review,

21:47.016 --> 21:49.266 align:middle
which obviously entails
some kind of debate

21:49.266 --> 21:52.496 align:middle
around allowing an overshoot
of the 2 percent target.

21:53.076 --> 21:55.786 align:middle
And in that-in that
context, is merely saying

21:55.786 --> 21:57.336 align:middle
that you want inflation to rise

21:57.336 --> 22:01.336 align:middle
above 2 percent a sufficient
strategy for getting inflation

22:01.336 --> 22:04.856 align:middle
to stay at the target, or
would it compel some kind

22:04.856 --> 22:07.746 align:middle
of policy action beyond
deferring future rate increases

22:08.266 --> 22:10.976 align:middle
to achieve that outcome
if that's the direction

22:10.976 --> 22:11.956 align:middle
in which the Committee goes?

22:11.956 --> 22:14.336 align:middle
In other words, it's one thing
to accept inflation rising

22:14.336 --> 22:16.226 align:middle
on its own opportunistically.

22:16.616 --> 22:18.906 align:middle
But if inflation does not
materialize, would a change

22:18.906 --> 22:21.146 align:middle
in the framework
necessarily make it easier

22:21.146 --> 22:22.546 align:middle
to generate higher inflation?

22:22.786 --> 22:23.316 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:23.316 --> 22:25.146 align:middle
Well, I-you know, I think
the answer to the question

22:25.146 --> 22:27.886 align:middle
of whether saying it is
enough to create credibility,

22:27.886 --> 22:28.936 align:middle
the answer to that is "no."

22:29.506 --> 22:34.026 align:middle
And I think you have to
back that up with policy

22:34.026 --> 22:36.226 align:middle
that supports the outcome, and
that's what we're trying to do.

22:36.746 --> 22:38.736 align:middle
And so the-the changes
that we're looking

22:38.736 --> 22:43.196 align:middle
at to the framework are-I think
they take all of that on board,

22:43.416 --> 22:45.816 align:middle
and-but they're-what
they're-what they are designed

22:45.816 --> 22:50.026 align:middle
to do is to strengthen
the credibility of that-of

22:50.026 --> 22:54.026 align:middle
that inflation target, but
only if followed by policy.

22:54.146 --> 22:55.666 align:middle
Ultimately, it will take time

22:55.666 --> 22:58.886 align:middle
to establish-to move
inflation expectations up from

22:58.886 --> 23:01.086 align:middle
where they are, which appears
to be a bit below 2 percent,

23:01.436 --> 23:02.416 align:middle
will not happen overnight.

23:02.416 --> 23:05.406 align:middle
It'll have to happen over
time as credibility is built.

23:06.176 --> 23:09.056 align:middle
You know, the Fed has great
inflation credibility,

23:09.816 --> 23:12.026 align:middle
but inflation expectations
are anchored

23:12.026 --> 23:13.726 align:middle
at about their 25-year average,

23:13.726 --> 23:16.036 align:middle
which is a few ticks
below 2 percent.

23:17.156 --> 23:18.046 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

23:18.876 --> 23:20.806 align:middle
We talked a lot about
inflation-Edward Lawrence

23:20.806 --> 23:21.746 align:middle
from Fox Business Network.

23:21.746 --> 23:23.246 align:middle
We talked a lot about inflation.

23:23.246 --> 23:25.086 align:middle
I want to ask you
about uncertainty.

23:25.086 --> 23:27.846 align:middle
With the House announcing
that they will ratify USMCA

23:27.846 --> 23:30.006 align:middle
and Canada and Mexico
signing off on it,

23:30.466 --> 23:32.486 align:middle
do you see that uncertainty
easing now

23:32.806 --> 23:35.976 align:middle
so the business investment
could pick up,

23:36.246 --> 23:39.636 align:middle
or are the trade tensions with
China just that big elephant

23:39.636 --> 23:42.816 align:middle
in the room where you might not
see those business investments

23:42.816 --> 23:43.146 align:middle
pick up?

23:43.146 --> 23:43.916 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:45.136 --> 23:46.876 align:middle
Well, I did see the news today

23:46.876 --> 23:50.626 align:middle
that it appears there's an
agreement to move forward

23:50.626 --> 23:53.966 align:middle
to vote on USMCA, and, of
course, it's not our role

23:53.966 --> 23:57.656 align:middle
to comment on particular
trade policies

23:57.656 --> 23:59.816 align:middle
or criticize them one way or
the other or evaluate them.

23:59.986 --> 24:03.796 align:middle
I will say, though, that
getting-if the deal were

24:03.796 --> 24:05.886 align:middle
to be enacted, then it
would certainly remove some

24:05.886 --> 24:08.196 align:middle
of the trade policy uncertainty,
and that would be, I believe,

24:08.196 --> 24:09.336 align:middle
a positive for the economy.

24:09.806 --> 24:14.076 align:middle
And I'll say the same thing
about the negotiations

24:14.076 --> 24:15.956 align:middle
with China, which haven't
reached that point yet.

24:17.076 --> 24:20.276 align:middle
We've been hearing
from our people

24:20.276 --> 24:22.716 align:middle
that we talked to-the many,
many people and businesses

24:22.716 --> 24:26.156 align:middle
that we talked to in-through
the Reserve Banks that wind

24:26.156 --> 24:27.706 align:middle
up being written up
in the Beige Book,

24:27.876 --> 24:29.846 align:middle
and they've been telling us
all year-for a year and a half,

24:29.846 --> 24:31.386 align:middle
really, that trade policy
uncertainty is weighing

24:31.386 --> 24:31.936 align:middle
on the outlook.

24:32.556 --> 24:35.006 align:middle
And I do think that,
again, without commenting

24:35.006 --> 24:38.646 align:middle
on it-in any way on the process
or the content of the agreement,

24:38.646 --> 24:41.346 align:middle
I think that uncertainty-removal
of uncertainty

24:41.346 --> 24:43.686 align:middle
around that would be a positive
for the economy as well.

24:43.686 --> 24:44.076 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

24:44.076 --> 24:45.266 align:middle
Is one uncertainty
bigger than the other?

24:45.266 --> 24:48.526 align:middle
Is-do you see-not commenting
on the deals themselves,

24:48.956 --> 24:50.426 align:middle
is one uncertainty-
CHAIR POWELL.

24:50.426 --> 24:53.516 align:middle
Well, I think you can-you
know, it's-I think you can see

24:53.516 --> 24:56.056 align:middle
that what's been moving-one
way to look at it is,

24:56.056 --> 24:57.506 align:middle
what's been moving
financial markets?

24:57.506 --> 24:59.956 align:middle
It's been news about
the negotiations

24:59.956 --> 25:01.516 align:middle
with China, not so much USMCA.

25:01.516 --> 25:07.166 align:middle
I think the difference between
NAFTA and USMCA is-is smaller

25:07.166 --> 25:11.486 align:middle
than the difference between
current, you know, arrangements

25:11.486 --> 25:12.926 align:middle
with China and what's
being negotiated.

25:14.296 --> 25:16.206 align:middle
MATTHEW BOESLER.

25:17.896 --> 25:19.746 align:middle
Hi, Matthew Boesler
at Bloomberg.

25:19.746 --> 25:24.096 align:middle
Chair Powell, the Fed policy
review this year has largely

25:24.096 --> 25:27.686 align:middle
been discussed in terms of the
inflation side of the mandate

25:27.846 --> 25:29.796 align:middle
and the need to shore up
inflation expectations.

25:29.796 --> 25:32.256 align:middle
But it seems like the
message you've been getting

25:32.256 --> 25:33.766 align:middle
from the Fed Listens events

25:34.106 --> 25:37.726 align:middle
and also something else you've
recently acknowledged is that,

25:37.976 --> 25:40.736 align:middle
you know, the Fed has also been
systematically overestimating

25:40.736 --> 25:42.896 align:middle
labor utilization
throughout this expansion.

25:43.326 --> 25:44.956 align:middle
So I'm wondering if you
could talk a little bit

25:44.956 --> 25:47.566 align:middle
about the extent to which
the review is looking

25:47.566 --> 25:49.566 align:middle
at the employment side
of the mandate as well.

25:50.006 --> 25:52.936 align:middle
Are there any possibilities,
you know,

25:52.996 --> 25:56.366 align:middle
for perhaps systematizing a more
asymmetric reaction function

25:56.746 --> 25:57.826 align:middle
with regard to unemployment?

25:58.116 --> 25:58.456 align:middle
Thank you.

25:58.736 --> 25:59.486 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:59.916 --> 26:02.696 align:middle
Yes. So the-you know, the
focus really is on how

26:02.696 --> 26:05.706 align:middle
to use our strategies,
tools, and communications

26:05.706 --> 26:07.036 align:middle
to achieve both sides
of the mandate.

26:07.036 --> 26:07.506 align:middle
And you're right, though,

26:07.506 --> 26:09.416 align:middle
that we've talked here
a lot about inflation.

26:10.146 --> 26:15.396 align:middle
And I think that the-I think,
more broadly, over a period

26:15.396 --> 26:20.926 align:middle
of years-many years-we've been
learning that the natural rate

26:20.926 --> 26:21.976 align:middle
of unemployment is lower.

26:21.976 --> 26:24.336 align:middle
It's just been-it's in
estimates not just at the Fed,

26:24.336 --> 26:28.066 align:middle
but among-broadly among
economists-labor economists have

26:28.066 --> 26:33.296 align:middle
seen that the connection-that
we can sustain much lower levels

26:33.296 --> 26:34.756 align:middle
of unemployment than
had been thought.

26:35.596 --> 26:37.416 align:middle
And, as I mentioned,
that's a good thing

26:37.416 --> 26:40.856 align:middle
because that means we-we
don't have to worry

26:40.856 --> 26:42.326 align:middle
so much about inflation.

26:42.576 --> 26:44.926 align:middle
And that's-and you see
the benefits of that

26:44.926 --> 26:45.946 align:middle
in today's labor market.

26:46.016 --> 26:51.816 align:middle
I-you know, the Fed Listens
events are about inflation

26:51.976 --> 26:53.516 align:middle
to a much lesser
extent than they are

26:53.516 --> 26:54.976 align:middle
about maximum employment.

26:54.976 --> 26:57.496 align:middle
If you've-if you've listened
to or attended any of those,

26:58.206 --> 27:01.446 align:middle
the discussion-we always
focus on inflation as well,

27:01.446 --> 27:03.636 align:middle
but the discussion is really
around what's happening in low-

27:03.636 --> 27:07.176 align:middle
and moderate-income communities
and among small businesses.

27:07.246 --> 27:10.076 align:middle
And I think you-you get a
perspective, which-you know,

27:10.076 --> 27:13.956 align:middle
we were already getting that
from our extensive outreach

27:13.956 --> 27:15.216 align:middle
to community groups and such.

27:15.216 --> 27:17.726 align:middle
But it was help to get-helpful
to get it in the context

27:17.726 --> 27:18.916 align:middle
of a monetary policy review.

27:19.576 --> 27:22.966 align:middle
And I think that the fact
that these communities have

27:22.966 --> 27:27.326 align:middle
so much-such high levels of
unemployment and low levels

27:27.326 --> 27:29.036 align:middle
of labor force participation
tells you

27:29.036 --> 27:30.276 align:middle
that there is slack out there.

27:30.276 --> 27:32.956 align:middle
And I think that does also
inform our understanding

27:32.956 --> 27:34.686 align:middle
of what we mean by
"maximum employment."

27:35.156 --> 27:36.626 align:middle
And it's been a very
positive-I would say

27:36.626 --> 27:40.866 align:middle
that the Fed Listens events have
been-have been extraordinarily

27:40.866 --> 27:43.926 align:middle
positive, and they're certainly
something we'll repeat.

27:44.976 --> 27:46.676 align:middle
You also see from the
business perspective

27:47.336 --> 27:52.126 align:middle
that companies are taking all
kinds of measures to-you know,

27:52.126 --> 27:56.286 align:middle
to look through things
on people's résumés

27:56.386 --> 27:59.216 align:middle
that would have perhaps
disqualified them in other kinds

27:59.216 --> 28:04.106 align:middle
of environments, training
is moving up-you hear just a

28:04.106 --> 28:06.256 align:middle
lot-there are a lot of
things going on that suggest

28:06.256 --> 28:09.086 align:middle
that people at the margins of
the labor force are being pulled

28:09.086 --> 28:12.686 align:middle
in and being given chances,
which is a great thing.

28:13.736 --> 28:14.276 align:middle
MATTHEW BOESLER.

28:14.796 --> 28:20.486 align:middle
Thanks. Sorry, if
I could just follow

28:20.486 --> 28:22.086 align:middle
up briefly-is there
any way to sort

28:22.086 --> 28:25.456 align:middle
of systematize these insights,
do you think, with, you know,

28:25.456 --> 28:27.996 align:middle
in terms of-we talk a lot about,
for example, makeup strategies

28:27.996 --> 28:28.906 align:middle
on the inflation side.

28:28.906 --> 28:31.106 align:middle
Is there anything similar you
could do on the employment side?

28:31.496 --> 28:32.106 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:32.246 --> 28:34.266 align:middle
You know, I-I think we have kind

28:34.266 --> 28:37.306 align:middle
of internalized-and this
isn't-this isn't news for today,

28:37.306 --> 28:39.566 align:middle
this is something we've
been saying for, you know,

28:39.566 --> 28:43.836 align:middle
for several years-you've seen
us moving down our estimates

28:43.836 --> 28:44.946 align:middle
of the-of maximum employment.

28:44.946 --> 28:47.886 align:middle
In fact, it's already-it's
already understood, I think,

28:48.026 --> 28:51.526 align:middle
that-that there's
more-even though we're

28:51.526 --> 28:54.346 align:middle
at 3½ percent unemployment,
there's actually more slack

28:54.346 --> 28:55.756 align:middle
out there, in a sense.

28:55.756 --> 28:59.136 align:middle
And-and the risks of, you know,

28:59.326 --> 29:02.076 align:middle
using accommodative
monetary policy, our tool,

29:02.806 --> 29:05.696 align:middle
to explore that are
relatively low.

29:05.696 --> 29:06.726 align:middle
And I think we know that,

29:07.356 --> 29:10.126 align:middle
and I think the review
certainly underscores that,

29:10.196 --> 29:13.916 align:middle
but that's-that's a
very important insight.

29:15.286 --> 29:16.566 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

29:16.566 --> 29:18.266 align:middle
Hi, Victoria Guida
with Politico.

29:18.266 --> 29:21.196 align:middle
I wanted to ask more
about the-the repo issues

29:21.196 --> 29:24.056 align:middle
and what you all are doing
beyond open market operations.

29:24.056 --> 29:29.406 align:middle
Are you currently, for
example, telling examiners not

29:29.626 --> 29:32.076 align:middle
to prefer reserves
over Treasuries

29:32.456 --> 29:34.966 align:middle
for supervisory purposes?

29:34.996 --> 29:37.296 align:middle
Are you talking to the
Treasury Department

29:37.296 --> 29:39.486 align:middle
about maybe reducing
the level of volatility

29:39.486 --> 29:40.606 align:middle
in their account at the Fed?

29:41.146 --> 29:44.716 align:middle
And on the standing repo
facility, is it, you know,

29:44.786 --> 29:48.406 align:middle
that you would be inclined
to do it, but you need

29:48.406 --> 29:49.886 align:middle
to figure out the details?

29:49.926 --> 29:52.686 align:middle
Or-or what would kind
of drive the decision

29:52.686 --> 29:53.716 align:middle
on whether or not to do that?

29:54.366 --> 29:55.536 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:55.886 --> 29:58.686 align:middle
So, on Treasuries
versus reserves,

29:59.716 --> 30:02.156 align:middle
we've done a ton
of work on that.

30:02.156 --> 30:04.716 align:middle
We've talked to supervisors,
and-it's interesting,

30:04.716 --> 30:07.096 align:middle
if you look at the banks,
they're all over the place

30:07.096 --> 30:09.686 align:middle
on the composition of
their-of their buffer.

30:09.796 --> 30:11.646 align:middle
So you have to have-you
have a business model,

30:11.646 --> 30:15.686 align:middle
and that business model suggests
what your stress outflows will

30:15.686 --> 30:18.246 align:middle
be, and that suggests what
your buffer should be.

30:18.316 --> 30:20.056 align:middle
And you see them making
quite different choices.

30:20.056 --> 30:23.206 align:middle
Some of them have lots of
reserves and, you know,

30:23.206 --> 30:25.186 align:middle
fewer Treasuries, and then
they change their mind

30:25.186 --> 30:26.956 align:middle
and they switch,
so it's not obvious

30:26.956 --> 30:28.626 align:middle
that there's one
thing happening there.

30:28.626 --> 30:30.736 align:middle
Notwithstanding that, we
have-we've gone out to try

30:30.736 --> 30:32.456 align:middle
to understand that,
talking to supervisors.

30:33.226 --> 30:39.186 align:middle
In terms of the-the TGA,
you know, we have not tried

30:39.186 --> 30:43.306 align:middle
to pull the TGA into this yet;
we've taken it as exogenous.

30:43.306 --> 30:45.756 align:middle
I don't know that at some point
we won't have those discussions,

30:45.796 --> 30:48.586 align:middle
but, you know, Treasury-we
want Treasury to be able

30:48.586 --> 30:49.966 align:middle
to have the cash that it needs.

30:50.606 --> 30:53.006 align:middle
And then we should-we
are essentially taking

30:53.006 --> 30:55.386 align:middle
that as exogenous
to our-to our work.

30:55.386 --> 30:59.396 align:middle
And, you know, there may come
a time when we-when we talk

30:59.396 --> 31:00.756 align:middle
about that, but we
haven't done much of that.

31:01.216 --> 31:04.226 align:middle
Standing repo facility-so
your question on that is,

31:04.226 --> 31:05.196 align:middle
what are we thinking about it?

31:05.196 --> 31:09.286 align:middle
What are we-I mean, I-so I
think we're-we are more focused,

31:09.366 --> 31:14.986 align:middle
frankly, on the T-the bill
purchases, the year-end,

31:15.466 --> 31:18.096 align:middle
and also the review
of supervisory

31:18.096 --> 31:21.666 align:middle
and regulatory issues
that we're digging into,

31:21.736 --> 31:24.926 align:middle
and-because we think, you know,

31:24.926 --> 31:28.176 align:middle
there's-these are structural
things-right?-where you could,

31:28.626 --> 31:30.516 align:middle
without sacrificing
safety and soundness,

31:30.516 --> 31:34.226 align:middle
just allow the-allow the
liquidity that is already there

31:34.226 --> 31:37.486 align:middle
to flow more freely, perhaps by
making fairly straightforward,

31:38.056 --> 31:39.536 align:middle
noncontroversial changes.

31:39.906 --> 31:41.026 align:middle
And we think there
is some of that,

31:41.066 --> 31:43.016 align:middle
though-and so we're
working hard and fast.

31:43.016 --> 31:45.836 align:middle
But those are things, you know,
the-if they take rule changes,

31:45.836 --> 31:47.336 align:middle
it'll take, you know, a notice

31:47.336 --> 31:49.396 align:middle
of public rulemaking-it'll
take three months

31:49.396 --> 31:50.116 align:middle
and things like that.

31:50.116 --> 31:51.866 align:middle
Those things take time.

31:51.866 --> 31:54.526 align:middle
These things that we're working
on now, though-like, you know,

31:54.526 --> 31:58.386 align:middle
going through year-end with
the overnight facilities

31:58.386 --> 32:01.926 align:middle
and the bill purchases and the
term repo-those are-those are

32:01.926 --> 32:03.686 align:middle
things we have to do
right now and are doing.

32:04.346 --> 32:05.656 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

32:08.166 --> 32:10.646 align:middle
Hi, Chris Rugaber
at Associated Press.

32:10.726 --> 32:14.126 align:middle
I wanted to ask, the only change
in the statement was a drop

32:14.126 --> 32:15.206 align:middle
in the reference

32:15.206 --> 32:18.416 align:middle
to uncertainties
around the economy.

32:18.676 --> 32:21.086 align:middle
You seem very confident
that-or it implies there's a lot

32:21.086 --> 32:23.516 align:middle
of confidence that those
uncertainties have gone away.

32:23.516 --> 32:27.606 align:middle
What caused you to make that
change for the statement?

32:27.766 --> 32:30.496 align:middle
What were you looking at that
seemed to be so much lower?

32:30.496 --> 32:31.176 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:31.176 --> 32:33.686 align:middle
Well, we did, actually-if
you look at the statement,

32:33.686 --> 32:38.606 align:middle
you'll notice that we-we did
call out global developments

32:38.606 --> 32:41.516 align:middle
and muted inflation pressures
later in the statement.

32:42.436 --> 32:43.376 align:middle
And why did we do that?

32:43.456 --> 32:45.296 align:middle
You know, those are the things

32:45.296 --> 32:46.806 align:middle
that we've been monitoring
all year.

32:47.246 --> 32:50.056 align:middle
We've put now in place policies
that we think are appropriate

32:50.056 --> 32:53.296 align:middle
to address those things, so
we're not revisiting that.

32:53.706 --> 32:55.336 align:middle
But those are-those
are just key things,

32:55.336 --> 32:57.516 align:middle
and they haven't gone away, so
we thought it was appropriate

32:57.516 --> 33:01.386 align:middle
to mention them there-still
subject to the idea that,

33:02.106 --> 33:04.426 align:middle
for us to change our
stance, we would want

33:04.426 --> 33:07.496 align:middle
to see a material
reassessment of the outlook.

33:07.496 --> 33:08.086 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

33:08.516 --> 33:09.546 align:middle
Well, just to follow real quick,

33:09.546 --> 33:12.086 align:middle
on the material-material
reassessment aspect,

33:12.086 --> 33:15.156 align:middle
are you worried that that
has set too high a bar

33:15.656 --> 33:17.606 align:middle
for potential cuts next year?

33:17.606 --> 33:19.016 align:middle
We were talking about
rate hikes,

33:19.146 --> 33:23.936 align:middle
but no-no Fed policymaker seems
to foresee any cuts next year.

33:23.936 --> 33:25.326 align:middle
Some economists do.

33:25.746 --> 33:28.316 align:middle
Does a material reassessment
mean you need

33:28.316 --> 33:30.816 align:middle
to see data actually worsen?

33:30.816 --> 33:33.726 align:middle
Does it reduce your ability
to act preemptively the way,

33:34.126 --> 33:35.156 align:middle
arguably, you did this year?

33:35.366 --> 33:35.626 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:35.916 --> 33:38.236 align:middle
So, I mean, one thing
that we're mindful of is

33:38.236 --> 33:41.096 align:middle
that we've cut rates
three times since July.

33:41.096 --> 33:43.166 align:middle
That's 75 basis points'
worth of cuts.

33:43.676 --> 33:48.966 align:middle
And we do believe that monetary
policy operates with long

33:48.966 --> 33:51.616 align:middle
and variable lags, and that it
will take some time before the

33:51.616 --> 33:55.786 align:middle
full effects of those actions
are seen in the economy.

33:55.946 --> 33:56.986 align:middle
So that'll take some time.

33:56.986 --> 33:59.586 align:middle
So that's one reason to
hold back and-and wait.

33:59.586 --> 34:02.156 align:middle
And we thought-I think
we took strong measures.

34:02.256 --> 34:05.426 align:middle
In fact, if you look
at-more broadly

34:05.526 --> 34:07.006 align:middle
at the Treasury yield curve,

34:07.276 --> 34:09.806 align:middle
it has moved more
than 75 basis points.

34:09.806 --> 34:11.776 align:middle
So you've-you've had
quite a significant move

34:11.776 --> 34:13.106 align:middle
in the direction of
higher accommodation.

34:13.386 --> 34:15.646 align:middle
In terms of what's-you know,
what is material at the end

34:15.646 --> 34:18.866 align:middle
of the day, well, I would just
say, whether or not a change

34:18.866 --> 34:21.866 align:middle
in the outlook merits a policy
response will be a collective

34:21.866 --> 34:22.926 align:middle
judgment of the FOMC.

34:23.456 --> 34:24.806 align:middle
There isn't any single factor

34:25.156 --> 34:26.416 align:middle
that will determine
our decisions.

34:26.416 --> 34:28.176 align:middle
We'll-we'll look at
a full range of data

34:28.176 --> 34:30.216 align:middle
and other information bearing
on the economic outlook.

34:32.276 --> 34:33.166 align:middle
SCOTT HORSLEY.

34:34.926 --> 34:38.436 align:middle
Thank you Mr. Chairman,
Scott Horsley for NPR.

34:38.436 --> 34:40.216 align:middle
You started out by
talking about Paul Volcker,

34:40.216 --> 34:42.736 align:middle
who obviously cast
a-a long shadow.

34:43.166 --> 34:46.736 align:middle
I wonder if, in hindsight,
that shadow was-was too long,

34:46.736 --> 34:50.086 align:middle
and if-in the decades since
his tenure and, in particular,

34:50.186 --> 34:55.106 align:middle
last year, if the Fed was too
quick to raise interest rates

34:55.796 --> 35:00.236 align:middle
to attack an inflation bogeyman
that didn't materialize.

35:00.726 --> 35:02.106 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:02.256 --> 35:04.906 align:middle
So I-this wouldn't be
a-my response will not be

35:04.906 --> 35:08.096 align:middle
about Paul Volcker,
but-so, well,

35:08.096 --> 35:09.906 align:middle
you started with Paul Volcker.

35:09.966 --> 35:12.606 align:middle
So, if you look back at
2018-I'll just take you back

35:12.606 --> 35:14.106 align:middle
to the beginning of 2018.

35:14.106 --> 35:17.866 align:middle
We had an economy growing at
3 percent, we had inflation

35:17.866 --> 35:20.716 align:middle
at 2 percent, and
we had a trillion

35:20.716 --> 35:23.076 align:middle
and a half dollars' worth
of stimulus arriving,

35:23.076 --> 35:25.326 align:middle
and the federal funds
rate was 1.4 percent.

35:26.056 --> 35:30.026 align:middle
So it wasn't that-so we
moved-we moved policy

35:30.026 --> 35:31.196 align:middle
up during the course
of the year.

35:31.496 --> 35:34.696 align:middle
We never got policy
even at the level

35:34.696 --> 35:36.916 align:middle
of what we thought the
neutral rate was at the time.

35:36.996 --> 35:39.016 align:middle
So there was no sense
of it being restrictive.

35:39.346 --> 35:42.686 align:middle
We took steps to make it less
accommodative, and that seemed

35:42.686 --> 35:43.656 align:middle
to be the right thing.

35:44.176 --> 35:46.776 align:middle
It still seems to me to be
the right thing in hindsight.

35:46.896 --> 35:49.126 align:middle
The idea that we were
trying to, you know,

35:49.126 --> 35:51.536 align:middle
slow the economy down-we
were really just trying

35:51.536 --> 35:53.046 align:middle
to get near neutral.

35:53.046 --> 35:55.436 align:middle
And even with the last
rate increase a year ago,

35:55.796 --> 35:58.756 align:middle
we were still meaningfully
below the median estimate

35:58.756 --> 36:00.236 align:middle
on the Committee of what
the neutral rate was.

36:00.236 --> 36:03.086 align:middle
So policy was always
accommodative during the course

36:03.086 --> 36:03.456 align:middle
of that year.

36:04.016 --> 36:06.056 align:middle
I think what's happened
is, the-obviously,

36:06.056 --> 36:07.876 align:middle
the facts on the
ground have changed.

36:08.386 --> 36:12.046 align:middle
You saw the global economic
slowdown begin in the middle

36:12.046 --> 36:17.776 align:middle
of last year, gather force,
and then continue to go

36:17.776 --> 36:19.976 align:middle
on this year, so you've
seen a continual weakening.

36:19.976 --> 36:22.906 align:middle
Just look at the IMF's forecasts
of growth from, you know,

36:22.906 --> 36:25.786 align:middle
the spring of 2018 and
compare them to now.

36:25.786 --> 36:27.766 align:middle
You've had quite a
significant-that does affect us.

36:28.086 --> 36:32.146 align:middle
I think, also, the trade
situation was just beginning

36:32.146 --> 36:35.826 align:middle
in the middle of '18, and I
think it had-has had-you know,

36:35.826 --> 36:38.376 align:middle
I think there'll be a very,
very wide range of estimates

36:38.376 --> 36:40.596 align:middle
of the effect, but I think
it's had a meaningful effect

36:40.596 --> 36:42.856 align:middle
on output just through the
uncertainty channel and,

36:42.856 --> 36:45.016 align:middle
to a lesser extent,
through-through,

36:45.016 --> 36:46.636 align:middle
you know, the tariff effects.

36:46.636 --> 36:48.506 align:middle
And, again, that's not
to make a judgment.

36:48.506 --> 36:51.276 align:middle
It's not up to us to make
a judgment about that.

36:51.676 --> 36:53.266 align:middle
So, that's what-that
would be my story.

36:54.686 --> 36:56.736 align:middle
DONNA BORAK.

36:56.866 --> 36:58.386 align:middle
Chair Powell, Donna
Borak with CNN.

36:58.866 --> 37:02.476 align:middle
To follow up on Edward's
trade question, could you talk

37:02.476 --> 37:06.326 align:middle
about what you would imagine
the economic effect would be

37:06.666 --> 37:09.636 align:middle
if negotiations with
China were to fall apart

37:09.636 --> 37:11.236 align:middle
and how the Fed might be able

37:11.236 --> 37:13.376 align:middle
to support the economy
in that event?

37:13.666 --> 37:14.056 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:14.166 --> 37:16.746 align:middle
You know, I wouldn't want to
speculate on-on a hypothetical.

37:16.746 --> 37:21.186 align:middle
I would say-we'll
just have to see.

37:21.186 --> 37:24.586 align:middle
We're-we look at
a range of factors

37:24.586 --> 37:26.726 align:middle
and we've-as I've said
before, we try to look

37:26.796 --> 37:30.966 align:middle
through the volatility in trade
news and trade negotiations.

37:30.966 --> 37:32.146 align:middle
We try not to react.

37:32.146 --> 37:32.936 align:middle
We can't react.

37:32.936 --> 37:35.766 align:middle
Monetary policy is not
the right tool to react

37:35.766 --> 37:39.866 align:middle
in the very short term to
volatility and, you know,

37:39.866 --> 37:41.806 align:middle
things that can change back
and forth and back and forth

37:41.806 --> 37:44.176 align:middle
as this has happened,
as is probably typical

37:44.176 --> 37:46.826 align:middle
of a-of a large complex
negotiation.

37:47.576 --> 37:52.046 align:middle
So, in terms of-you know,
I-again, I don't want to get

37:52.096 --> 37:54.546 align:middle
into hypothetical outcomes,
though, if that's all right.

37:55.576 --> 37:56.596 align:middle
DONNA BORAK.

37:56.596 --> 37:59.886 align:middle
I'm just wondering, you met
with the President last month.

37:59.886 --> 38:03.096 align:middle
Did you have any advice for
him when it came to this,

38:03.096 --> 38:04.616 align:middle
and did you express
some concerns

38:04.616 --> 38:06.696 align:middle
about the potential
volatility and the impact

38:06.696 --> 38:08.476 align:middle
of the economy on-given
all this?

38:08.876 --> 38:09.326 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:09.596 --> 38:11.086 align:middle
You know, I-I'm going
to stick with my

38:11.296 --> 38:14.516 align:middle
and my predecessors'
longtime practice

38:14.516 --> 38:16.936 align:middle
of not discussing
private meetings

38:16.936 --> 38:18.756 align:middle
with elected officials-or
other officials, really.

38:19.206 --> 38:19.616 align:middle
But thank you.

38:19.676 --> 38:24.016 align:middle
VIRGINIE MONTET.

38:24.876 --> 38:25.406 align:middle
Thank you.

38:25.876 --> 38:27.926 align:middle
Virginie Montet with
Agence France-Presse.

38:28.786 --> 38:30.576 align:middle
Yesterday, Democratic leaders

38:30.576 --> 38:34.506 align:middle
in the House have launched
an impeachment process

38:34.506 --> 38:35.416 align:middle
against the President.

38:36.136 --> 38:38.176 align:middle
Have you mentioned
these developments

38:38.176 --> 38:40.616 align:middle
within the Committee, as-today

38:40.616 --> 38:42.936 align:middle
as potentially presenting
some risks

38:42.936 --> 38:44.596 align:middle
for their confidence
in the economy?

38:45.486 --> 38:45.726 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:45.726 --> 38:48.126 align:middle
No. We have not.

38:48.126 --> 38:51.226 align:middle
We don't consider
things like that.

38:51.456 --> 38:51.946 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

38:52.506 --> 38:56.926 align:middle
Mike Derby with Dow
Jones Newswires.

38:56.926 --> 39:00.436 align:middle
Does the Fed's dot plot still
serve a useful communications

39:00.626 --> 39:04.176 align:middle
purpose, or do you feel that
it might be time to retire it

39:04.176 --> 39:05.536 align:middle
or change it in some fashion?

39:06.206 --> 39:06.576 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:06.576 --> 39:09.356 align:middle
With-I think properly
understood, it can be useful,

39:10.006 --> 39:11.846 align:middle
but that's been a challenge.

39:11.846 --> 39:15.966 align:middle
You know, I think "properly
understood" to me means looking

39:15.966 --> 39:18.086 align:middle
at what it is and
not at what it isn't.

39:18.086 --> 39:22.006 align:middle
And what it is is, it's an
expression of the thinking

39:22.006 --> 39:24.176 align:middle
about individual
Committee members

39:25.326 --> 39:28.076 align:middle
about appropriate
monetary policy

39:28.076 --> 39:29.076 align:middle
and the path of the economy.

39:29.576 --> 39:31.066 align:middle
Remember that we
write all that down,

39:31.066 --> 39:34.176 align:middle
and we send it in,
and it gets compiled.

39:34.176 --> 39:35.646 align:middle
But we don't discuss
it at the meeting,

39:35.646 --> 39:36.706 align:middle
and we don't negotiate a plan.

39:36.706 --> 39:38.116 align:middle
There is no-there's
no agreement.

39:38.116 --> 39:38.936 align:middle
There's no plan.

39:39.286 --> 39:42.556 align:middle
And I think, particularly
at inflection points,

39:43.066 --> 39:47.426 align:middle
it's hard to convey the reality,

39:47.426 --> 39:49.846 align:middle
which is that policy is
always going to depend

39:50.306 --> 39:53.356 align:middle
on the economic outlook and
changes in the economic outlook.

39:53.356 --> 39:55.016 align:middle
And when the economic
outlook is changing,

39:55.466 --> 39:57.996 align:middle
the dots are-they're
just not a consideration.

39:57.996 --> 40:00.276 align:middle
We're going to do what we
think is the right thing

40:00.276 --> 40:00.906 align:middle
for the economy.

40:00.906 --> 40:03.046 align:middle
And if the fact-dots that
we did six months ago

40:03.046 --> 40:05.086 align:middle
or three months ago
don't agree with that,

40:05.126 --> 40:06.506 align:middle
that's not even in
the conversation.

40:06.626 --> 40:11.196 align:middle
So its more-but it can it be
useful if-and I think-but,

40:11.196 --> 40:14.866 align:middle
as I said, it's been a
challenge, and so I-I do-I do

40:14.866 --> 40:17.036 align:middle
like to say, if you focus
too much on the dots,

40:17.036 --> 40:18.736 align:middle
you can-you can miss
the broader picture.

40:20.036 --> 40:21.266 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

40:22.476 --> 40:26.056 align:middle
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

40:27.376 --> 40:31.656 align:middle
Chairman Powell, many people
seem worried that the framework

40:31.866 --> 40:34.156 align:middle
that you guys are
working on is going

40:34.156 --> 40:38.186 align:middle
to be-the results are going
to be less rather than more.

40:38.796 --> 40:40.686 align:middle
And I-people keep coming back

40:40.686 --> 40:44.996 align:middle
and saying why-asking
why you took off, like,

40:44.996 --> 40:48.796 align:middle
a 4 percent inflation rate off
the table even when it started.

40:48.796 --> 40:51.716 align:middle
I mean, as you said,
going around the country

40:51.716 --> 40:52.896 align:middle
for all the Listen events,

40:53.406 --> 40:55.626 align:middle
I don't think I heard
anybody worry

40:55.626 --> 40:57.936 align:middle
about a 4 percent
inflation rate or think

40:57.936 --> 40:59.886 align:middle
that a higher inflation rate
was going to be a problem.

41:00.276 --> 41:02.746 align:middle
So where does that
concern come from?

41:03.036 --> 41:05.796 align:middle
Is it members of Congress
that have-have said this?

41:06.696 --> 41:07.216 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:07.296 --> 41:12.186 align:middle
No. So let me say, we're-we've
been working on this all year.

41:12.366 --> 41:14.596 align:middle
And we're just at the stage

41:14.596 --> 41:17.456 align:middle
where we've had a really
interesting discussion

41:17.456 --> 41:20.096 align:middle
about the various tools that
we have at the October meeting.

41:20.736 --> 41:22.006 align:middle
At this meeting, we talked

41:22.006 --> 41:25.276 align:middle
about the way monetary policy
affects different groups

41:25.276 --> 41:25.926 align:middle
in the economy.

41:25.926 --> 41:29.056 align:middle
So we had a-we talked about
the Fed Listens events

41:29.056 --> 41:31.816 align:middle
and some very interesting
research.

41:32.406 --> 41:34.786 align:middle
So we're just getting
to the stage

41:34.786 --> 41:37.316 align:middle
where we're looking
at conclusions.

41:37.316 --> 41:38.926 align:middle
You know, what do we
take away from all this?

41:38.926 --> 41:43.256 align:middle
And those things-many of those
things would wind up as changes,

41:43.256 --> 41:45.226 align:middle
if you will, modifications
to the "Statement

41:45.226 --> 41:47.226 align:middle
of Longer-Run Goals and
Monetary Policy Strategy."

41:47.226 --> 41:50.956 align:middle
I think that process will
take until the middle

41:50.956 --> 41:52.446 align:middle
of the year where-but we want

41:52.446 --> 41:54.296 align:middle
to approach it very
thoroughly and very carefully.

41:54.296 --> 41:56.966 align:middle
And that is-in effect, that
is our framework document.

41:57.346 --> 41:58.486 align:middle
And I wouldn't prejudge.

41:58.486 --> 42:03.986 align:middle
No one-I believe we will be
able to-to reach a successful

42:03.986 --> 42:05.866 align:middle
conclusion and make
meaningful improvements.

42:05.926 --> 42:10.096 align:middle
I do. In terms of "4 percent"-so
that's-I think it's premature

42:10.096 --> 42:12.196 align:middle
for people to be saying that
this isn't going anywhere.

42:13.016 --> 42:14.856 align:middle
You know-and if you
define going anywhere

42:14.856 --> 42:17.226 align:middle
as a 4 percent inflation
target-let me talk

42:17.226 --> 42:20.096 align:middle
about the 4 percent
inflation target.

42:21.176 --> 42:23.986 align:middle
So I'll go back to the point

42:24.766 --> 42:28.586 align:middle
that just saying words
is not itself credible.

42:28.716 --> 42:31.566 align:middle
So I think if you said, "We're
raising the inflation target

42:31.566 --> 42:34.276 align:middle
to 4," what would be
the effect of that?

42:34.276 --> 42:36.046 align:middle
I mean, where's the
credibility in that, really?

42:36.416 --> 42:38.366 align:middle
You haven't been
able to get it to 2.

42:38.836 --> 42:40.356 align:middle
So I think we-I think you need

42:40.356 --> 42:41.636 align:middle
to lower your sights
a little bit.

42:41.636 --> 42:44.626 align:middle
I also think, is 4 percent-you'd
have to ask the question,

42:44.626 --> 42:47.326 align:middle
is that really, you
know, price stability?

42:47.816 --> 42:49.026 align:middle
Is that really price stability?

42:49.026 --> 42:50.646 align:middle
Is that within our
legal mandate?

42:50.686 --> 42:53.506 align:middle
I mean, I think it's
a fair question.

42:54.866 --> 43:00.846 align:middle
So, you know, I think-I'd
like for this review to come

43:00.846 --> 43:03.666 align:middle
out with very-a set of
positive results-you know,

43:03.666 --> 43:04.996 align:middle
meaningful improvements.

43:05.296 --> 43:08.606 align:middle
It doesn't mean it has to solve
every problem going forward.

43:08.726 --> 43:11.206 align:middle
We want to-we want to have
this be a successful exercise

43:11.206 --> 43:14.726 align:middle
where we meaningfully improve
our monetary policy framework.

43:15.106 --> 43:18.186 align:middle
These things don't tend
to move in-you know,

43:18.256 --> 43:20.276 align:middle
in a lurchy way,
they tend to evolve.

43:20.516 --> 43:24.076 align:middle
But I think-and if we do
this, then in a few years,

43:24.076 --> 43:25.716 align:middle
again and again and
things like that,

43:25.716 --> 43:28.036 align:middle
then we can-at least we're
moving in a good direction.

43:28.036 --> 43:28.756 align:middle
And I think we will be.

43:28.916 --> 43:32.336 align:middle
I'm confident we will be.

43:32.936 --> 43:38.196 align:middle
DON LEE. Don Lee
with the L.A. Times.

43:38.966 --> 43:41.406 align:middle
Chair Powell, you've had a
busy year, and I'm wondering,

43:41.816 --> 43:45.576 align:middle
as you look back, what things
you might have done differently.

43:45.676 --> 43:48.646 align:middle
And are there any lessons that
you would take into next year?

43:50.306 --> 43:51.166 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

43:51.166 --> 43:55.486 align:middle
Well, you know, I have to say,
my total focus is on right now

43:55.616 --> 43:58.606 align:middle
and getting policy
right and thinking

43:58.606 --> 43:59.756 align:middle
about next year-you know,

43:59.756 --> 44:01.646 align:middle
thinking about what's the
economy going to be doing.

44:02.296 --> 44:04.126 align:middle
I like where we are on
policy, as I mentioned.

44:04.126 --> 44:07.226 align:middle
I think our policy stance is
appropriate and likely to remain

44:07.226 --> 44:09.236 align:middle
so as long as the outlook
is broadly like this.

44:10.286 --> 44:15.746 align:middle
You know-I mean, it's
too long of a question.

44:15.846 --> 44:18.046 align:middle
You know, I don't know
how to get after that.

44:18.046 --> 44:19.706 align:middle
There's-there's a
lot of learning

44:19.706 --> 44:22.776 align:middle
that comes into-from
the economy every year

44:23.266 --> 44:24.596 align:middle
and in the way we do our jobs.

44:25.096 --> 44:26.066 align:middle
And, you know, we're
always going

44:26.066 --> 44:26.936 align:middle
to be trying to learn lessons.

44:27.646 --> 44:29.906 align:middle
DON LEE. I mean, are there
any particular surprises,

44:30.446 --> 44:32.706 align:middle
whether it's the
economy or how markets

44:32.706 --> 44:35.726 align:middle
or financial- CHAIR POWELL.

44:35.726 --> 44:36.306 align:middle
Well, I-so, yes.

44:36.306 --> 44:40.416 align:middle
I didn't-obviously,
you know, didn't see

44:40.416 --> 44:45.166 align:middle
and I don't think anybody
saw coming the-the challenges

44:45.166 --> 44:46.186 align:middle
that we faced this year.

44:47.006 --> 44:49.926 align:middle
I think they were a surprise.

44:49.926 --> 44:54.396 align:middle
I think that, toward
the end of 2018,

44:54.396 --> 44:56.136 align:middle
there was still a sense
the economy was growing

44:56.136 --> 44:57.156 align:middle
at around 3 percent.

44:57.356 --> 45:01.876 align:middle
And I didn't expect
the-to face the challenges,

45:01.876 --> 45:05.886 align:middle
but I think we did face them,
and I think we-I'm pleased

45:05.886 --> 45:08.926 align:middle
that we moved to support the
economy in the way that we did.

45:09.036 --> 45:11.936 align:middle
I think our moves will
prove appropriate.

45:12.456 --> 45:15.696 align:middle
And, again, I think
both the economy

45:15.696 --> 45:19.706 align:middle
and monetary policy right now,
I think, are in a good place.

45:20.666 --> 45:23.376 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

45:23.636 --> 45:25.686 align:middle
I'm Nancy Marshall-Genzer
from Marketplace.

45:25.996 --> 45:29.246 align:middle
Chair Powell, why aren't we
seeing stronger wage gains?

45:29.656 --> 45:32.186 align:middle
Wages are growing
more slowly now

45:32.186 --> 45:34.116 align:middle
than they were toward the
beginning of the year.

45:34.116 --> 45:34.836 align:middle
Why is that?

45:35.586 --> 45:36.506 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

45:37.746 --> 45:41.256 align:middle
Well, wage gains
have moved up a bit.

45:41.256 --> 45:43.926 align:middle
If you look back
three, four years,

45:43.926 --> 45:45.786 align:middle
you'll see wages are
growing around 2 percent.

45:45.846 --> 45:50.216 align:middle
Now you see them moving up, you
know, more at 3, 3½ percent.

45:50.346 --> 45:52.886 align:middle
So why aren't they growing
higher, at a faster rate?

45:53.296 --> 45:54.476 align:middle
And it's a couple of things.

45:54.576 --> 45:56.736 align:middle
I think there are a
range of explanations.

45:56.846 --> 46:00.946 align:middle
For instance, one would be that
productivity has just been low.

46:01.076 --> 46:04.556 align:middle
So wages should go up to cover
inflation and productivity.

46:04.556 --> 46:07.856 align:middle
Productivity has been low,
and that-that is very likely

46:07.856 --> 46:09.346 align:middle
to be holding back wages.

46:10.276 --> 46:15.216 align:middle
I also think there are other
possible potential explanations,

46:15.216 --> 46:18.566 align:middle
such as, you know,
globalization can be-the idea

46:18.606 --> 46:21.306 align:middle
that you can make, manufacture,

46:21.436 --> 46:24.656 align:middle
or even provide services
anywhere in the world

46:25.086 --> 46:26.106 align:middle
to anywhere in the world.

46:26.106 --> 46:29.086 align:middle
I think that hangs over the
wage-setting process-and

46:29.676 --> 46:30.636 align:middle
everywhere, pretty much.

46:30.636 --> 46:33.576 align:middle
You don't see-there isn't
the kind of traction

46:33.926 --> 46:36.676 align:middle
in the wage market that-even
in a tight labor market.

46:36.916 --> 46:39.686 align:middle
Another thing is, though, that
the labor market may not be

46:39.686 --> 46:41.826 align:middle
as tight as we had
thought it was.

46:42.086 --> 46:47.366 align:middle
And, you know, I think
there-there are many,

46:47.366 --> 46:49.046 align:middle
many possible explanations.

46:49.506 --> 46:51.726 align:middle
I will say, though, if
you look, for example,

46:51.726 --> 46:59.836 align:middle
at nonsupervisory employees
in the labor report,

46:59.836 --> 47:02.226 align:middle
there are-their wages are
going up at 3.7 percent.

47:03.166 --> 47:06.726 align:middle
And so, you do see-and wages
are going up the most for people

47:06.726 --> 47:09.746 align:middle
at the lower end of the-that's
been true for the last couple

47:09.746 --> 47:11.076 align:middle
of years-lower end
of the wage spectrum.

47:11.076 --> 47:12.946 align:middle
So you do see wages moving up.

47:13.296 --> 47:16.096 align:middle
It's just-they're not moving
up at-at very high rates.

47:16.236 --> 47:18.736 align:middle
And, again, at the end of the
day, that probably has most

47:18.736 --> 47:19.596 align:middle
to do with productivity.

47:20.066 --> 47:21.636 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

47:22.136 --> 47:24.086 align:middle
Can I ask a quick follow-up?

47:24.486 --> 47:28.356 align:middle
Just as far as the market not
being as tight as you thought,

47:28.356 --> 47:30.866 align:middle
are you saying there are still
more people on the sidelines

47:30.866 --> 47:33.436 align:middle
who could join the labor force?

47:33.436 --> 47:34.006 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

47:34.006 --> 47:34.406 align:middle
Well, yes.

47:34.406 --> 47:38.846 align:middle
And I would also say that
we-if you ask people,

47:38.966 --> 47:41.376 align:middle
and we did ask people, what
do you think the natural rate

47:41.376 --> 47:42.116 align:middle
of unemployment is?

47:42.286 --> 47:44.416 align:middle
People were writing down
numbers in the fives,

47:44.416 --> 47:46.446 align:middle
and then they were writing
down numbers in the fours.

47:46.446 --> 47:48.836 align:middle
And now, unemployment has
been in the threes for a year

47:48.836 --> 47:51.266 align:middle
and a half, and we still
see wage inflation,

47:51.266 --> 47:51.926 align:middle
as you mentioned.

47:52.446 --> 47:56.006 align:middle
The level of wage inflation has
actually moved down, although,

47:56.006 --> 47:57.976 align:middle
there may be compositional
effects in that number

47:57.976 --> 47:59.916 align:middle
that may-that may
be, to some extent,

47:59.916 --> 48:01.716 align:middle
about younger workers coming

48:01.716 --> 48:03.736 align:middle
in at lower wages
that-than retiring workers.

48:03.736 --> 48:05.496 align:middle
But you wouldn't-that
shouldn't have much

48:05.496 --> 48:07.426 align:middle
of an effect, actually.

48:08.046 --> 48:09.446 align:middle
So why is that?

48:09.446 --> 48:12.146 align:middle
It may just be that there's
more slack in the economy.

48:12.276 --> 48:13.606 align:middle
And I think we are seeing that.

48:13.606 --> 48:16.036 align:middle
We're seeing-really, it showed
up through higher participation.

48:16.696 --> 48:18.326 align:middle
For many years, we've thought

48:18.326 --> 48:20.356 align:middle
that there's a trend
decline in participation.

48:20.876 --> 48:23.006 align:middle
Notwithstanding that,
against that trend,

48:23.006 --> 48:25.796 align:middle
we've seen prime-age
participation moving

48:25.796 --> 48:28.046 align:middle
up pretty steadily over the
last two or three years.

48:28.766 --> 48:31.306 align:middle
And it's a very positive
thing, but it does sort

48:31.306 --> 48:32.496 align:middle
of provide more labor supply,

48:33.326 --> 48:34.506 align:middle
meaning a less tight
labor market.

48:36.246 --> 48:37.466 align:middle
HANNAH LANG.

48:38.766 --> 48:40.596 align:middle
Hi. Hannah Lang with
American Banker.

48:40.686 --> 48:42.046 align:middle
Thanks for being here today.

48:42.126 --> 48:44.666 align:middle
I just wanted to ask about the
Community Reinvestment Act,

48:44.756 --> 48:49.176 align:middle
since the FDIC and OCC may
potentially join together

48:49.176 --> 48:51.226 align:middle
with a-for a proposal
without the Fed.

48:51.226 --> 48:54.356 align:middle
Are you concerned that
this might cause confusion,

48:54.356 --> 48:55.786 align:middle
and how would the mechanics work

48:55.786 --> 48:58.176 align:middle
if this proposal is
finalized without the Fed?

48:58.696 --> 48:59.496 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

48:59.666 --> 49:02.886 align:middle
So, you know, we think the
CRA-we know the CRA is a very

49:02.886 --> 49:05.016 align:middle
important law, and
we're strongly committed

49:05.016 --> 49:09.096 align:middle
to the mission of ensuring
that banks provide credit

49:09.096 --> 49:11.416 align:middle
to their-throughout
their communities,

49:11.416 --> 49:13.216 align:middle
particularly addressing
the needs of low-

49:13.216 --> 49:15.256 align:middle
and moderate-income
households and neighborhoods.

49:16.086 --> 49:17.886 align:middle
We also think it's
time for modernization.

49:17.886 --> 49:20.836 align:middle
We've thought that for some
time, and we worked very hard

49:20.996 --> 49:25.956 align:middle
to try to get aligned with the
OCC, really, on-on a proposal.

49:26.626 --> 49:28.696 align:middle
And my hope is that
we can still do that.

49:29.146 --> 49:31.956 align:middle
You know, I don't know whether
that'll be possible or not.

49:31.956 --> 49:33.496 align:middle
It will have to-well,
we'll just have to see,

49:33.556 --> 49:35.866 align:middle
but-and if we-if we can't,

49:35.866 --> 49:37.886 align:middle
I don't-I'm not sure what
the path forward would be.

49:37.886 --> 49:45.746 align:middle
We would certainly not want to
create confusion or, you know,

49:45.986 --> 49:49.166 align:middle
sort of tension between
the regimes if they do turn

49:49.166 --> 49:50.586 align:middle
out to be slightly
different regimes.

49:50.886 --> 49:52.616 align:middle
So that's something we-I
hope we don't have to face,

49:52.656 --> 49:55.476 align:middle
but we will if we have to.

49:55.476 --> 49:57.406 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

49:57.586 --> 49:59.576 align:middle
Hi there. Brian Cheung
with Yahoo Finance.

49:59.576 --> 50:02.986 align:middle
So, before the July meeting, you
said something kind of colorful.

50:02.986 --> 50:04.656 align:middle
You said, "To call something
'hot,' you would need

50:04.656 --> 50:06.676 align:middle
to see some heat,"
referring to the labor market.

50:06.676 --> 50:08.846 align:middle
So to expand a bit, I
guess, on Nancy's question,

50:08.846 --> 50:11.496 align:middle
it seems like the wage Phillips
curve has been pretty well

50:11.496 --> 50:14.216 align:middle
explored already, but
what would you need to see

50:14.216 --> 50:16.356 align:middle
to call the labor market
"hot" in that case?

50:16.356 --> 50:19.166 align:middle
Would it be a contract-or,
rather, some sort of change

50:19.166 --> 50:20.796 align:middle
in either the headline
number of gains

50:20.796 --> 50:21.616 align:middle
or in the unemployment rate?

50:21.616 --> 50:22.346 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

50:22.466 --> 50:23.296 align:middle
Really, wages.

50:23.426 --> 50:26.356 align:middle
I mean, we-there's so many
other measures that suggest

50:26.356 --> 50:27.786 align:middle
that the labor market is-I

50:27.786 --> 50:29.336 align:middle
like to say the labor
market is strong.

50:29.966 --> 50:32.586 align:middle
I don't really want to
say that it's tight.

50:32.586 --> 50:33.676 align:middle
Someone asked me a question

50:33.676 --> 50:34.766 align:middle
about a hot labor
market-that was

50:34.766 --> 50:35.846 align:middle
in the Humphrey-Hawkins
hearings.

50:36.426 --> 50:38.506 align:middle
So I'll say that the
labor market is strong.

50:38.506 --> 50:39.546 align:middle
I don't know that it's tight,

50:39.546 --> 50:42.376 align:middle
because you're not seeing
wage increases, you know.

50:42.376 --> 50:45.016 align:middle
Ultimately, if it's tight, those
should be-should be reflected

50:45.016 --> 50:49.566 align:middle
in higher wage increases, so
it does come down to that.

50:49.676 --> 50:53.196 align:middle
You know, we look at
countless measures

50:53.196 --> 50:56.526 align:middle
of labor market-you
know, labor utilization,

50:56.526 --> 50:59.386 align:middle
and there's so many-it's
too many to count.

50:59.946 --> 51:02.486 align:middle
But the one that has-that
is kind of suggesting

51:02.486 --> 51:04.636 align:middle
that you're-it's a healthy
number that, you know,

51:04.636 --> 51:07.776 align:middle
the sort of 3.1 percent average
hourly earnings number is a

51:07.776 --> 51:10.986 align:middle
decent number-3.7
percent for production

51:10.986 --> 51:12.786 align:middle
and nonsupervisory
workers is more healthy.

51:13.646 --> 51:17.216 align:middle
Ultimately, though, we'd
like to see-to call it "hot,"

51:17.486 --> 51:19.296 align:middle
you'd want to see heat.

51:19.456 --> 51:21.526 align:middle
You'd want to see, you
know, higher wages.

51:24.436 --> 51:25.036 align:middle
Thanks.

